# ============================================================================
# d3_ipv6_padn.rules  --  Suricata companion rule for the D3 defense
#
# HONEST SCOPING (read before using in the paper or on a resume):
#   * This rule is a PRACTICAL APPROXIMATION of the exact D3 predicate. Suricata
#     has no first-class "PadN option content" keyword, so it cannot cleanly
#     express "a PadN option contains a non-zero byte." The precise predicate is
#     implemented in d3_replay.py; THIS rule is the recognizable, deployable
#     approximation.
#   * It targets the NAIVE channel only. The adaptive channel sets PadN=0 and
#     mimics benign Flow-Label/IAT; no static signature detects it. Report both:
#     "Rule A catches the naive channel; no static rule catches the adaptive one."
#   * Coverage/FP numbers MUST come from your own replay (see commands below).
#     Do not cite any number you have not measured.
#
# Rule A1 (structural): alert on an IPv6 Destination Options extension header
#   present on a flow masquerading as TLS/443. This is coarse: it fires on the
#   PRESENCE of the Destination Options EH, which the covert channel always adds
#   and which real backbone IPv6 essentially never uses (0.0000% in our MAWI
#   trace). Tune/inspect before deployment.
alert ip any any -> any 443 ( \
    msg:"D3 IPv6 Destination-Options EH present on TLS port (possible PadN covert channel)"; \
    decode-event:ipv6.exthdr_useless_fh; \
    ipproto:ipv6; \
    sid:3100001; rev:1; \
    metadata:policy research, channel naive; \
)

# Rule A2 (byte-content, best-effort): match the Destination Options next-header
#   pattern in the IPv6 header chain. The Destination Options EH type is 60; a
#   preceding Next Header byte of 0x3c (60) indicates its presence. This is a
#   heuristic content match and may need adjustment for your capture's layout;
#   validate against d3_replay.py rather than trusting it blind.
alert ipv6 any any -> any any ( \
    msg:"D3 IPv6 Next Header = Destination Options (0x3c) heuristic"; \
    content:"|3c|"; offset:6; depth:1; \
    sid:3100002; rev:1; \
    metadata:policy research, channel naive; \
)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPLAY + COUNT (fill numbers from these, do not fabricate):
#
#   suricata -r malicious_raw.pcap -S d3_ipv6_padn.rules -l ./suri_covert --runmode single
#   suricata -r mawi_trace.pcap    -S d3_ipv6_padn.rules -l ./suri_benign --runmode single
#
#   # count alerts (detections on covert; false positives on benign):
#   jq -r 'select(.event_type=="alert") | .alert.signature_id' suri_covert/eve.json | sort | uniq -c
#   jq -r 'select(.event_type=="alert") | .alert.signature_id' suri_benign/eve.json | sort | uniq -c
#
# Report: alerts on covert (detection) vs alerts on benign (false positives),
# per signature id, alongside the exact d3_replay.py numbers.
# ============================================================================
